WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.319
Rust code can be correct in isolation and
still fail operationally.

2
00:00:03.399 --> 00:00:05.278
Async systems make this especially
visible.

3
00:00:05.358 --> 00:00:08.671
Anti-patterns include unbounded task
spawning, queues with no backpressure,

4
00:00:08.751 --> 00:00:11.569
retries without limits, missing timeouts,
ignored cancellation,

5
00:00:11.649 --> 00:00:14.666
and cleanup behavior that depends on tasks
finishing in a perfect order.

6
00:00:14.746 --> 00:00:17.070
Locks also need attention in async and
concurrent code.

7
00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:19.474
Holding a lock across unrelated work, slow
operations,

8
00:00:19.554 --> 00:00:22.472
or code that can wait indefinitely can
create availability problems.

9
00:00:22.552 --> 00:00:25.024
The defensive pattern is to keep critical
sections narrow,

10
00:00:25.104 --> 00:00:27.033
define cancellation behavior, set
timeouts,

11
00:00:27.113 --> 00:00:28.843
and make queue and retry limits explicit.

12
00:00:28.923 --> 00:00:31.396
Dependency governance is another Rust
anti-pattern area.

13
00:00:31.476 --> 00:00:34.987
Adding crates casually, enabling broad
feature flags, ignoring lockfile changes,

14
00:00:35.067 --> 00:00:38.380
or failing to review build scripts and
procedural macros can expand the trusted

15
00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:39.152
computing base.

16
00:00:39.232 --> 00:00:41.903
A dependency may bring parsers, network
behavior, native code,

17
00:00:41.983 --> 00:00:44.653
optional protocols, or transitive crates
that deserve review.

18
00:00:44.733 --> 00:00:48.293
Logging and tracing should support
operations without leaking sensitive material.

19
00:00:48.373 --> 00:00:51.489
Logs should avoid secrets, tokens,
credentials, private configuration,

20
00:00:51.569 --> 00:00:53.299
personal data, and excessive internals.

21
00:00:53.379 --> 00:00:56.890
Structured logging is useful, but fields
still need classification and redaction.

22
00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:59.690
Debug-friendly output should not become
permanent disclosure.

23
00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:03.034
Operational blind spots include missing
metrics, unclear readiness checks,

24
00:01:03.114 --> 00:01:04.844
weak error reporting, no release review,

25
00:01:04.924 --> 00:01:08.040
and tests that stop at unit-level behavior
while ignoring runtime limits.

26
00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:11.235
Defensive Rust treats the running system
as part of the security boundary.

27
00:01:11.315 --> 00:01:13.837
Observability, configuration review,
dependency review,

28
00:01:13.917 --> 00:01:16.340
and release discipline are part of the
code's trust story.
